1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,991
Last time, I left you with a puzzle from a courtroom.

2
00:00:03,991 --> 00:00:05,660
Today, we solve it.

3
00:00:06,010 --> 00:00:08,482
Picture a city of eight million people.

4
00:00:08,482 --> 00:00:12,745
One night, a crime. At the scene, police find a tiny trace of

5
00:00:12,745 --> 00:00:13,190
DNA.

6
00:00:13,540 --> 00:00:17,396
It's only a partial trace, so it can't point to one person.

7
00:00:17,396 --> 00:00:18,470
But it's still rare.

8
00:00:18,820 --> 00:00:21,651
Weeks later, police find a man.

9
00:00:21,651 --> 00:00:25,080
Let's call him Sam. Sam's DNA matches the trace.

10
00:00:25,430 --> 00:00:29,322
In court, an expert explains: if you picked an innocent person

11
00:00:29,322 --> 00:00:33,010
at random, the chance they would match is just one in a million.

12
00:00:33,360 --> 00:00:37,033
Then the prosecutor stands up and says: so there's only a one in

13
00:00:37,033 --> 00:00:39,070
a million chance that Sam is innocent.

14
00:00:39,870 --> 00:00:43,000
The jury nods. One in a million sounds like proof.

15
00:00:43,350 --> 00:00:45,081
Take a second, and decide.

16
00:00:45,081 --> 00:00:46,960
Do you agree with the prosecutor?

17
00:00:48,460 --> 00:00:51,104
If you said yes, you're in good company.

18
00:00:51,104 --> 00:00:55,070
Judges, juries and even experts have made this exact mistake.

19
00:00:55,420 --> 00:00:58,576
By the end of this video, you'll see that Sam is much more

20
00:00:58,576 --> 00:01:00,683
likely innocent than guilty.

21
00:01:00,683 --> 00:01:03,210
And you'll be able to spot this trap anywhere.

22
00:01:03,560 --> 00:01:05,320
It's called the prosecutor's fallacy.

23
00:01:06,320 --> 00:01:07,808
Welcome to Math Lab TV.

24
00:01:07,808 --> 00:01:09,930
Where math is something you can see.

25
00:01:10,630 --> 00:01:13,177
Today you will learn why the chance of the evidence,

26
00:01:13,177 --> 00:01:16,660
if someone is innocent, is not the chance they are innocent.

27
00:01:17,010 --> 00:01:20,660
You will see how to count the crowd, and find how many innocent

28
00:01:20,660 --> 00:01:22,380
people share the same evidence.

29
00:01:22,730 --> 00:01:25,702
And you will learn a simple recipe to catch this swap in

30
00:01:25,702 --> 00:01:28,420
courtrooms, medical tests, and the news.

31
00:01:29,020 --> 00:01:32,664
In our hypothesis testing video, we flipped a friend's coin

32
00:01:32,664 --> 00:01:35,030
twenty times, and got fifteen heads.

33
00:01:35,380 --> 00:01:39,009
We found that a fair coin would do something that extreme only

34
00:01:39,009 --> 00:01:41,270
about two times in a hundred.

35
00:01:41,620 --> 00:01:44,777
And we warned: that is not the chance the coin is fair.

36
00:01:44,777 --> 00:01:46,460
The question had been flipped.

37
00:01:47,260 --> 00:01:49,110
The courtroom is the very same flip.

38
00:01:49,110 --> 00:01:50,960
So let's write it down carefully.

39
00:01:51,310 --> 00:01:55,276
Mathematicians write the chance of something with a capital P.

40
00:01:55,276 --> 00:01:58,826
And they use a short vertical bar, which you can read as the

41
00:01:58,826 --> 00:01:59,670
word given.

42
00:02:00,020 --> 00:02:04,126
So P of match, given innocent, means: suppose the person is

43
00:02:04,126 --> 00:02:06,720
innocent. How likely is a match?

44
00:02:07,070 --> 00:02:08,844
That's the number the expert gave.

45
00:02:08,844 --> 00:02:10,230
One in a million.

46
00:02:10,830 --> 00:02:13,073
But the jury needs a different number.

47
00:02:13,073 --> 00:02:14,984
P of innocent, given match.

48
00:02:14,984 --> 00:02:16,491
Suppose the person matches.

49
00:02:16,491 --> 00:02:18,550
How likely are they innocent?

50
00:02:18,900 --> 00:02:21,015
Look at the two boxes. Same words.

51
00:02:21,015 --> 00:02:22,700
Just swapped around the bar.

52
00:02:23,700 --> 00:02:27,385
The prosecutor took the first number, and read it out as the

53
00:02:27,385 --> 00:02:29,990
second. That swap is the whole fallacy.

54
00:02:31,190 --> 00:02:32,913
So what should the expert have said?

55
00:02:32,913 --> 00:02:35,421
Something like this: if Sam were innocent,

56
00:02:35,421 --> 00:02:37,520
a match would be very unlikely.

57
00:02:37,870 --> 00:02:41,271
That sentence is true. But it does not tell you how likely Sam

58
00:02:41,271 --> 00:02:45,310
is to be innocent. To get that, you need one more ingredient.

59
00:02:46,110 --> 00:02:47,998
Is swapping really such a big deal?

60
00:02:47,998 --> 00:02:49,700
Let's try it on something simple.

61
00:02:50,050 --> 00:02:52,507
What's the chance that a dog has four legs?

62
00:02:52,507 --> 00:02:55,680
Almost certain. Nearly every dog does.

63
00:02:56,030 --> 00:02:59,540
Now swap it. What's the chance that something with four legs is

64
00:02:59,540 --> 00:03:00,230
a dog?

65
00:03:00,580 --> 00:03:02,780
Not very likely at all.

66
00:03:02,780 --> 00:03:05,915
Cats have four legs. So do cows, and horses.

67
00:03:05,915 --> 00:03:07,620
Even your kitchen table.

68
00:03:08,620 --> 00:03:11,912
The two numbers share the same words, but they can be wildly

69
00:03:11,912 --> 00:03:12,790
different.

70
00:03:13,140 --> 00:03:15,025
The difference comes from the crowd.

71
00:03:15,025 --> 00:03:18,460
How many other things, besides dogs, also have four legs?

72
00:03:19,460 --> 00:03:20,984
So why do smart people swap them?

73
00:03:20,984 --> 00:03:22,881
Because our brains love a simple story.

74
00:03:22,881 --> 00:03:26,268
The evidence fits the guilty person, so the guilty person must

75
00:03:26,268 --> 00:03:27,420
fit the evidence.

76
00:03:27,770 --> 00:03:30,966
But fitting the evidence is easy when the crowd is big.

77
00:03:30,966 --> 00:03:33,170
Lots of innocent people can fit it too.

78
00:03:33,970 --> 00:03:37,124
In Sam's case, the crowd is a city of eight million people.

79
00:03:37,124 --> 00:03:38,120
So let's count it.

80
00:03:38,470 --> 00:03:41,760
Before we start, let's be honest about what we are assuming.

81
00:03:42,110 --> 00:03:45,370
First, the person who did it lives somewhere in this city.

82
00:03:45,720 --> 00:03:49,140
Second, the real culprit's DNA always matches the trace.

83
00:03:49,490 --> 00:03:52,649
And third, for now, we know nothing else about Sam.

84
00:03:52,649 --> 00:03:55,380
Only the match. We'll come back to that one.

85
00:03:56,180 --> 00:03:57,829
Here is the whole city.

86
00:03:57,829 --> 00:04:01,382
Eight million people, drawn as eight big blocks of one million

87
00:04:01,382 --> 00:04:01,840
each.

88
00:04:02,190 --> 00:04:03,740
One of them is the culprit.

89
00:04:03,740 --> 00:04:06,453
That leaves seven million, nine hundred and ninety nine

90
00:04:06,453 --> 00:04:09,220
thousand, nine hundred and ninety nine innocent people.

91
00:04:09,570 --> 00:04:10,852
Now the expert's number.

92
00:04:10,852 --> 00:04:14,260
Each innocent person matches with a chance of one in a million.

93
00:04:14,610 --> 00:04:18,038
So in a block of one million innocent people,

94
00:04:18,038 --> 00:04:21,220
how many would we expect to match by pure chance?

95
00:04:21,570 --> 00:04:22,240
About one.

96
00:04:23,240 --> 00:04:24,925
And we have eight of those blocks.

97
00:04:24,925 --> 00:04:28,493
So across the whole city, about eight innocent people match the

98
00:04:28,493 --> 00:04:29,770
trace. Just by luck.

99
00:04:30,970 --> 00:04:32,194
You can check it yourself.

100
00:04:32,194 --> 00:04:35,030
Eight million, times one in a million, is eight.

101
00:04:35,830 --> 00:04:38,840
Plus the one real culprit, who matches for sure.

102
00:04:39,190 --> 00:04:42,407
So if police lined up everyone in the city who matches,

103
00:04:42,407 --> 00:04:44,390
there would be about nine people in that line.

104
00:04:45,390 --> 00:04:47,440
And only one of those nine is guilty.

105
00:04:48,440 --> 00:04:50,339
Sam is one of the people in that line.

106
00:04:50,339 --> 00:04:51,903
All we know is that he matched.

107
00:04:51,903 --> 00:04:54,810
So the chance he's the guilty one is about one in nine.

108
00:04:56,010 --> 00:04:59,170
Which means the chance he's innocent is about eight in nine.

109
00:04:59,170 --> 00:05:00,860
Around eighty nine percent.

110
00:05:02,360 --> 00:05:04,950
Notice what we did. No fancy formula.

111
00:05:04,950 --> 00:05:08,885
We just imagined everyone in the city, and asked who else would

112
00:05:08,885 --> 00:05:09,300
match.

113
00:05:09,650 --> 00:05:11,480
Put the two numbers next to each other.

114
00:05:11,830 --> 00:05:15,140
The chance of a match, given innocent: one in a million.

115
00:05:15,490 --> 00:05:19,240
The chance of innocent, given a match: about eight in nine.

116
00:05:19,590 --> 00:05:22,828
The second number is almost nine hundred thousand times bigger.

117
00:05:22,828 --> 00:05:25,000
That's how far apart the two questions are.

118
00:05:26,500 --> 00:05:29,850
What we just did is count, but you can write it as a short rule.

119
00:05:30,200 --> 00:05:34,246
The chance of guilt, given a match, equals the guilty people who

120
00:05:34,246 --> 00:05:37,330
match, divided by everyone who matches.

121
00:05:37,680 --> 00:05:41,576
On top, one. On the bottom, one plus eight.

122
00:05:41,576 --> 00:05:42,550
One ninth.

123
00:05:43,350 --> 00:05:46,385
In our Bayes theorem video, we wrote this same idea as a

124
00:05:46,385 --> 00:05:49,861
formula. Here, counting the crowd gets you to the same answer,

125
00:05:49,861 --> 00:05:51,770
with no formula to remember.

126
00:05:52,120 --> 00:05:53,994
And notice where the eight came from.

127
00:05:53,994 --> 00:05:56,076
Not from the expert's number alone.

128
00:05:56,076 --> 00:05:57,950
It came from the size of the crowd.

129
00:05:58,950 --> 00:06:00,537
That crowd size has a name.

130
00:06:00,537 --> 00:06:02,124
It's called the base rate.

131
00:06:02,124 --> 00:06:04,850
And it's the piece the prosecutor left out.

132
00:06:05,200 --> 00:06:07,599
Let's keep the same one in a million trace,

133
00:06:07,599 --> 00:06:08,810
and just change the crowd.

134
00:06:09,160 --> 00:06:12,953
Imagine the crime happened in a tiny village of one thousand

135
00:06:12,953 --> 00:06:15,390
people, and nobody else could have been there.

136
00:06:15,740 --> 00:06:19,282
Now the chance that even one innocent villager matches is about

137
00:06:19,282 --> 00:06:20,620
one in a thousand.

138
00:06:20,970 --> 00:06:24,021
So if someone in the village matches, they are almost certainly

139
00:06:24,021 --> 00:06:26,915
the culprit. About nine hundred and ninety nine times in a

140
00:06:26,915 --> 00:06:27,450
thousand.

141
00:06:28,450 --> 00:06:31,828
Back in our city of eight million, the same match gave about one

142
00:06:31,828 --> 00:06:32,420
in nine.

143
00:06:32,770 --> 00:06:35,650
And if anyone in a whole country of sixty million could have

144
00:06:35,650 --> 00:06:38,250
done it, about sixty innocent people would match.

145
00:06:38,250 --> 00:06:41,170
The chance of guilt drops to about one in sixty one.

146
00:06:42,370 --> 00:06:44,321
The evidence never changed.

147
00:06:44,321 --> 00:06:48,006
Only the crowd did. And the answer swung from almost certain,

148
00:06:48,006 --> 00:06:49,740
to almost certainly not.

149
00:06:51,240 --> 00:06:52,725
Same trace. Same expert.

150
00:06:52,725 --> 00:06:56,720
Same one in a million. Three completely different answers.

151
00:06:57,720 --> 00:07:00,267
That's why the expert's number, on its own,

152
00:07:00,267 --> 00:07:02,384
can never tell you the chance of guilt.

153
00:07:02,384 --> 00:07:04,290
You always need the crowd as well.

154
00:07:04,640 --> 00:07:07,204
Now, remember our third assumption.

155
00:07:07,204 --> 00:07:10,020
We knew nothing about Sam except the match.

156
00:07:10,370 --> 00:07:12,978
In a real case, there's usually more.

157
00:07:12,978 --> 00:07:15,150
Maybe Sam was seen near the scene that night.

158
00:07:15,500 --> 00:07:18,026
Other evidence works by shrinking the crowd.

159
00:07:18,026 --> 00:07:20,133
Instead of eight million possible people,

160
00:07:20,133 --> 00:07:22,450
maybe only a few hundred were nearby.

161
00:07:22,800 --> 00:07:26,134
In a smaller crowd, far fewer innocent people match by luck,

162
00:07:26,134 --> 00:07:28,670
and the match becomes much stronger evidence.

163
00:07:29,670 --> 00:07:31,170
So the match isn't useless.

164
00:07:31,170 --> 00:07:34,901
It's one piece, and it has to be combined with everything else,

165
00:07:34,901 --> 00:07:35,670
honestly.

166
00:07:36,470 --> 00:07:39,638
In fact, here's how much the match moved things.

167
00:07:39,638 --> 00:07:43,302
Before it, Sam was just one of eight million people.

168
00:07:43,302 --> 00:07:45,010
One in eight million.

169
00:07:45,360 --> 00:07:47,386
After it, he's one in nine.

170
00:07:47,386 --> 00:07:50,580
His chance of guilt jumped almost nine hundred thousand times.

171
00:07:51,580 --> 00:07:55,330
Strong evidence. Just not, on its own, proof.

172
00:07:56,130 --> 00:07:57,711
This isn't just a classroom puzzle.

173
00:07:57,711 --> 00:08:01,651
In England, in nineteen ninety nine, a mother was convicted

174
00:08:01,651 --> 00:08:04,010
after two of her babies died suddenly.

175
00:08:04,360 --> 00:08:08,162
A medical expert told the jury that, for a family like hers,

176
00:08:08,162 --> 00:08:11,336
the chance of one sudden infant death was about one in eight

177
00:08:11,336 --> 00:08:12,490
thousand five hundred.

178
00:08:12,840 --> 00:08:15,249
He then multiplied that number by itself,

179
00:08:15,249 --> 00:08:18,633
and said the chance of two such deaths was about one in seventy

180
00:08:18,633 --> 00:08:19,450
three million.

181
00:08:19,800 --> 00:08:21,451
That number had two problems.

182
00:08:21,451 --> 00:08:24,958
First, multiplying assumes the two deaths were unrelated.

183
00:08:24,958 --> 00:08:28,654
But families can share genes and homes, so a second death is

184
00:08:28,654 --> 00:08:30,510
more likely after a first.

185
00:08:30,860 --> 00:08:35,016
Second, the swap. One in seventy three million was presented as

186
00:08:35,016 --> 00:08:37,300
if it were the chance she was innocent.

187
00:08:38,300 --> 00:08:41,860
But a double murder of babies is also extremely rare.

188
00:08:41,860 --> 00:08:45,421
The real question is which rare explanation is more likely.

189
00:08:45,421 --> 00:08:47,520
Comparing them points the other way.

190
00:08:48,520 --> 00:08:51,460
Statisticians spoke out, and in two thousand and three,

191
00:08:51,460 --> 00:08:53,410
her conviction was overturned.

192
00:08:54,910 --> 00:08:58,296
The very same swap happens in doctors' offices,

193
00:08:58,296 --> 00:08:59,490
every day.

194
00:08:59,840 --> 00:09:03,190
Back in our Bayes theorem video, a test gave a false alarm to

195
00:09:03,190 --> 00:09:04,780
one in ten healthy people.

196
00:09:05,130 --> 00:09:07,668
That's the chance of a positive, given healthy.

197
00:09:07,668 --> 00:09:11,870
Swap it, and ask: given a positive, how likely are you healthy?

198
00:09:12,220 --> 00:09:15,463
Out of a thousand people, ninety nine healthy people got false

199
00:09:15,463 --> 00:09:18,505
alarms, and only nine people who were really sick tested

200
00:09:18,505 --> 00:09:19,330
positive.

201
00:09:19,680 --> 00:09:22,274
So eleven out of twelve of the positives were healthy.

202
00:09:22,274 --> 00:09:23,830
A world away from one in ten.

203
00:09:25,030 --> 00:09:26,617
It's the courtroom again.

204
00:09:26,617 --> 00:09:29,790
A rare false alarm, inside a big healthy crowd,

205
00:09:29,790 --> 00:09:32,060
still adds up to lots of false alarms.

206
00:09:33,060 --> 00:09:36,435
That's why a single positive result is usually followed by a

207
00:09:36,435 --> 00:09:38,000
second, different test.

208
00:09:38,000 --> 00:09:41,032
A second test shrinks the crowd, just like extra evidence in

209
00:09:41,032 --> 00:09:41,440
court.

210
00:09:42,240 --> 00:09:45,217
So if you ever get a scary test result, ask the flipped

211
00:09:45,217 --> 00:09:47,724
question. Out of everyone who tests positive,

212
00:09:47,724 --> 00:09:49,150
how many really have it?

213
00:09:50,150 --> 00:09:53,145
Once you can see this swap, you'll find it everywhere,

214
00:09:53,145 --> 00:09:55,330
especially in the world of data.

215
00:09:55,680 --> 00:09:59,227
In machine learning, take a face recognition camera at a

216
00:09:59,227 --> 00:10:02,774
stadium, looking for one wanted person among fifty thousand

217
00:10:02,774 --> 00:10:03,190
fans.

218
00:10:03,540 --> 00:10:06,801
It only wrongly matches one face in ten thousand.

219
00:10:06,801 --> 00:10:09,845
Sounds great. But fifty thousand fans means about five false

220
00:10:09,845 --> 00:10:12,240
matches, plus the one real person.

221
00:10:12,590 --> 00:10:16,941
So when the alarm goes off, it's the right person only about one

222
00:10:16,941 --> 00:10:17,850
time in six.

223
00:10:18,850 --> 00:10:22,170
In data science, it's the p value from our coin video.

224
00:10:22,170 --> 00:10:25,048
A p value is the chance of results this extreme,

225
00:10:25,048 --> 00:10:27,040
given that nothing is going on.

226
00:10:27,390 --> 00:10:29,780
It is not the chance that nothing is going on.

227
00:10:29,780 --> 00:10:32,566
If only one coin in a hundred were rigged,

228
00:10:32,566 --> 00:10:35,525
that coin would still be fair about eighty three times in a

229
00:10:35,525 --> 00:10:36,140
hundred.

230
00:10:37,140 --> 00:10:40,131
In finance, a bank's fraud model flags card payments.

231
00:10:40,131 --> 00:10:43,540
It wrongly flags just one honest payment in a thousand.

232
00:10:43,890 --> 00:10:47,602
But for every million honest payments, there are maybe a hundred

233
00:10:47,602 --> 00:10:50,176
frauds. The model catches ninety of those,

234
00:10:50,176 --> 00:10:52,530
and also flags a thousand honest ones.

235
00:10:52,880 --> 00:10:56,190
So only about one flag in twelve is really fraud.

236
00:10:56,190 --> 00:10:59,280
The rest are customers whose cards just got blocked.

237
00:11:00,280 --> 00:11:03,160
And in computer science, it's what happens when you search a

238
00:11:03,160 --> 00:11:04,140
huge database.

239
00:11:04,490 --> 00:11:08,038
Search five million DNA profiles for a one in a million match,

240
00:11:08,038 --> 00:11:10,411
and you'd expect about five innocent hits,

241
00:11:10,411 --> 00:11:12,980
even if the real culprit isn't in there at all.

242
00:11:13,330 --> 00:11:16,726
The bigger the search, the more coincidences you're guaranteed

243
00:11:16,726 --> 00:11:17,370
to find.

244
00:11:18,370 --> 00:11:20,816
Four different fields, one same trap.

245
00:11:20,816 --> 00:11:23,707
A rare false match, multiplied by a big crowd,

246
00:11:23,707 --> 00:11:25,710
gives you lots of false matches.

247
00:11:26,710 --> 00:11:28,927
Let's practise. I'll read four claims.

248
00:11:28,927 --> 00:11:33,360
For each one, decide: is it a swap, or is it fair?

249
00:11:33,710 --> 00:11:37,466
Claim one. A smoke alarm almost always beeps when there's a

250
00:11:37,466 --> 00:11:41,220
fire. So when it beeps, there's almost certainly a fire.

251
00:11:42,720 --> 00:11:46,044
That's a swap. Think of every single time the alarm beeps.

252
00:11:46,044 --> 00:11:48,750
Most of those times, someone just burnt the toast.

253
00:11:49,550 --> 00:11:53,990
Claim two. Nearly every professional basketball player is tall.

254
00:11:53,990 --> 00:11:57,975
So if you're tall, you're probably a professional basketball

255
00:11:57,975 --> 00:11:58,430
player.

256
00:11:59,930 --> 00:12:02,426
Swap again. There are millions of tall people,

257
00:12:02,426 --> 00:12:05,450
and only a few hundred of them play professionally.

258
00:12:06,250 --> 00:12:09,960
Claim three. Out of everyone in the city who matched the trace,

259
00:12:09,960 --> 00:12:12,020
about one in nine is guilty.

260
00:12:13,520 --> 00:12:16,416
That one is fair. It starts from the people who match,

261
00:12:16,416 --> 00:12:18,478
and asks how many are guilty.

262
00:12:18,478 --> 00:12:20,950
That's exactly the question the jury needs.

263
00:12:21,950 --> 00:12:26,073
Claim four. A spam filter catches nine out of ten spam emails.

264
00:12:26,073 --> 00:12:29,240
So nine out of ten emails it blocks are spam.

265
00:12:30,740 --> 00:12:34,388
Swap. To know how many blocked emails are spam,

266
00:12:34,388 --> 00:12:37,816
you'd need to know how many real emails it blocks too.

267
00:12:37,816 --> 00:12:39,270
That's the crowd again.

268
00:12:40,070 --> 00:12:42,660
Let's name the mistakes, so you can catch them.

269
00:12:43,010 --> 00:12:45,093
Mistake one: the swap itself.

270
00:12:45,093 --> 00:12:48,409
Reading the chance of the evidence, given innocence,

271
00:12:48,409 --> 00:12:51,520
as the chance of innocence, given the evidence.

272
00:12:51,870 --> 00:12:53,348
Listen for it in the words.

273
00:12:53,348 --> 00:12:56,264
One in a million chance an innocent person would match,

274
00:12:56,264 --> 00:12:59,360
is not the same as one in a million chance he's innocent.

275
00:13:00,360 --> 00:13:02,471
Mistake two: forgetting the crowd.

276
00:13:02,471 --> 00:13:06,023
A rare match means very different things in a village and in a

277
00:13:06,023 --> 00:13:06,870
whole country.

278
00:13:07,220 --> 00:13:09,076
Mistake three goes the other way.

279
00:13:09,076 --> 00:13:11,970
Some defense lawyers say: there are nine matches,

280
00:13:11,970 --> 00:13:14,849
so the chance he did it is only one in nine.

281
00:13:14,849 --> 00:13:16,500
The evidence is worthless.

282
00:13:16,850 --> 00:13:20,234
That's a fallacy too. The match took eight million people down

283
00:13:20,234 --> 00:13:22,708
to nine. That's powerful evidence.

284
00:13:22,708 --> 00:13:24,510
It just isn't proof on its own.

285
00:13:25,510 --> 00:13:29,067
And a bonus mistake, from the real case: multiplying chances

286
00:13:29,067 --> 00:13:31,781
together when the events are connected.

287
00:13:31,781 --> 00:13:34,910
That can make a coincidence look far rarer than it is.

288
00:13:35,710 --> 00:13:38,570
Here's a recipe to catch the swap in any claim you're shown.

289
00:13:38,920 --> 00:13:40,714
Step one: find the given.

290
00:13:40,714 --> 00:13:43,450
Ask what the number assumes is already true.

291
00:13:43,800 --> 00:13:46,636
Step two: flip it. Write down the question you actually care

292
00:13:46,636 --> 00:13:49,300
about, and check it's not the one you were given.

293
00:13:49,650 --> 00:13:51,203
Step three: find the crowd.

294
00:13:51,203 --> 00:13:55,202
How many people, or tests, or payments, could have produced this

295
00:13:55,202 --> 00:13:56,090
evidence?

296
00:13:56,440 --> 00:13:58,528
Step four: count the matches.

297
00:13:58,528 --> 00:14:01,990
Expected false matches, plus the real ones.

298
00:14:02,340 --> 00:14:06,136
And step five: divide. The real matches, out of all the matches.

299
00:14:06,136 --> 00:14:08,300
Then add in any other evidence.

300
00:14:09,300 --> 00:14:12,636
Try it on Sam. The given: innocent.

301
00:14:12,636 --> 00:14:16,239
Flip it: is Sam innocent, given the match?

302
00:14:16,239 --> 00:14:18,017
The crowd: eight million.

303
00:14:18,017 --> 00:14:21,085
The matches: about eight, plus one.

304
00:14:21,085 --> 00:14:23,130
Divide: one in nine.

305
00:14:24,130 --> 00:14:26,708
Because a rare match is not the same as a rare chance of

306
00:14:26,708 --> 00:14:27,480
innocence.

307
00:14:28,980 --> 00:14:32,499
Look around your world, and notice how often a rare coincidence

308
00:14:32,499 --> 00:14:33,750
gets treated as proof.

309
00:14:34,350 --> 00:14:36,413
If this lesson made things clear for you,

310
00:14:36,413 --> 00:14:39,296
please subscribe to Math Lab TV, so we can keep building

311
00:14:39,296 --> 00:14:40,130
together.

312
00:14:40,930 --> 00:14:42,390
But here's a puzzle for next time.

313
00:14:42,740 --> 00:14:45,340
A model says it catches ninety percent of fraud.

314
00:14:45,340 --> 00:14:48,280
Another says ninety percent of its alarms are real.

315
00:14:48,280 --> 00:14:50,200
Are they bragging about the same thing?

316
00:14:50,550 --> 00:14:53,259
They're not. It's the same swap we saw today,

317
00:14:53,259 --> 00:14:57,101
wearing a lab coat. Next time, we'll lay it all out in one

318
00:14:57,101 --> 00:14:59,810
simple table, called the confusion matrix.

319
00:15:00,160 --> 00:15:01,520
I will see you in the next video.
